WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.106
Welcome to Module 7,
the last module of the

2
00:00:03.106 --> 00:00:07.150
Ticket-to-Machine learning
path: evidence for the ISSO.

3
00:00:07.150 --> 00:00:12.538
This module covers the guide's Gate 7
and the developer workbook's Chapter 07.

4
00:00:12.538 --> 00:00:18.375
In Module 6, a finding closed only on a
re-read, with its evidence on the record.

5
00:00:18.375 --> 00:00:23.400
Now you turn those closed findings
into the evidence an ISSO keeps.

6
00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:26.900
You'll learn what Gate 7 runs
on a schedule and reports.

7
00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:31.775
Then you'll export a period's closed
findings as the Federal Modernization

8
00:00:31.775 --> 00:00:36.728
Workbook's Book 13's evidence records,
chain one period to the next, and verify

9
00:00:36.728 --> 00:00:41.838
the package the way an ISSO would,
including catching a record someone removed.

10
00:00:41.838 --> 00:00:46.074
If you're an ISSO reviewer,
this module is yours in full.

11
00:00:46.074 --> 00:00:50.460
The code is illustrative, tested
against fictional fixtures.

12
00:00:54.210 --> 00:00:55.360
Why does this matter?

13
00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:00.347
At the end of the month, your ISSO
needs to show an assessor three things.

14
00:01:00.347 --> 00:01:05.210
That every identity finding was
owned, clocked, re-read and closed.

15
00:01:05.210 --> 00:01:07.485
That nothing was edited after it closed.

16
00:01:07.485 --> 00:01:09.597
And that nothing was quietly dropped.

17
00:01:09.597 --> 00:01:12.498
Ticket history and
screenshots don't prove that.

18
00:01:12.498 --> 00:01:15.197
A ticket shows a status, not a re-read.

19
00:01:15.197 --> 00:01:18.183
And neither one can show an
edit that left no trace,

20
00:01:18.183 --> 00:01:20.148
or a record that's simply missing.

21
00:01:20.148 --> 00:01:24.567
In this module you learn what Gate
7 runs and reports, then build and

22
00:01:24.567 --> 00:01:29.910
verify the evidence package for a period,
a package that can prove all three.

23
00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:36.475
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module,

24
00:01:36.475 --> 00:01:39.098
which takes about forty-five
minutes with the lab.

25
00:01:39.098 --> 00:01:43.285
You'll explain what Gate 7 schedules
and reports to the governance board.

26
00:01:43.285 --> 00:01:46.142
You'll describe Book 13's evidence record,

27
00:01:46.142 --> 00:01:49.335
which of its fields Pass
1 leaves empty, and why.

28
00:01:49.335 --> 00:01:52.273
You'll produce an evidence
package for a period,

29
00:01:52.273 --> 00:01:54.835
and continue its hash chain into the next.

30
00:01:54.835 --> 00:01:56.434
And you'll verify a package,

31
00:01:56.434 --> 00:02:00.360
and explain how the verifier
catches an edited or removed record.

32
00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:02.935
Before you start, finish Module 6.

33
00:02:02.935 --> 00:02:06.706
If you're an ISSO reviewer
working only this module,

34
00:02:06.706 --> 00:02:11.735
read Module 1's Unit 3 first, on the
identity finding and its contract.

35
00:02:11.735 --> 00:02:13.435
Lab 1 needs no tenant.

36
00:02:13.435 --> 00:02:16.139
Lab 2 needs your developer instance,

37
00:02:16.139 --> 00:02:20.210
with at least one finding
closed in Module 6's Lab 2.

38
00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:27.510
Once the loop runs, it
runs on Book 13's tempo.

39
00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:28.610
P1 halts.

40
00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:34.110
P2 has one hour, P3 twenty-four
hours, and P4 seventy-two hours.

41
00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:37.248
Those are the program's
windows, not federal ones.

42
00:02:37.248 --> 00:02:40.348
Gate 7 is how you operate
it, in five steps.

43
00:02:40.348 --> 00:02:43.495
Schedule the drift reconciliation
and the re-read client,

44
00:02:43.495 --> 00:02:46.773
and keep their outputs with the
date and the checker version.

45
00:02:46.773 --> 00:02:49.335
Report five measures to
the governance board.

46
00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:51.885
Route the identity fixes through the loop.

47
00:02:51.885 --> 00:02:54.747
Export the evidence in Book 13's shape.

48
00:02:54.747 --> 00:03:00.010
And review each declared exception at its
end date, and each L4 class quarterly.

49
00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:04.905
The guide's Gate 7 validation names
the export as its automated check,

50
00:03:04.905 --> 00:03:07.910
and Chapter 07 builds its ServiceNow half.

51
00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:09.910
That's what you'll run in the lab.

52
00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:18.766
Gate 7 reports five measures to the
governance board, from Book 13's Chapter 10:

53
00:03:18.766 --> 00:03:22.435
detection coverage, window
adherence, escalation rate,

54
00:03:22.435 --> 00:03:26.185
machine-closure share, and
the declared-exception count.

55
00:03:26.185 --> 00:03:28.085
How is Gate 7 validated?

56
00:03:28.085 --> 00:03:30.322
The automated checks are read-only.

57
00:03:30.322 --> 00:03:33.477
The evidence export writes
and checks the records,

58
00:03:33.477 --> 00:03:38.210
and the drift comparison from Module
4 classifies findings across two runs.

59
00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:41.498
The five measures themselves
have no automated check yet.

60
00:03:41.498 --> 00:03:46.449
The manual validation is that the scheduled
runs have run for a month with outputs

61
00:03:46.449 --> 00:03:50.308
kept, the measures have been
reported once, and the first monthly

62
00:03:50.308 --> 00:03:54.022
package has been assembled end
to end and accepted by the ISSO.

63
00:03:54.022 --> 00:03:56.282
Until the export exists in your estate,

64
00:03:56.282 --> 00:03:59.247
the interim path is to
assemble the package by hand,

65
00:03:59.247 --> 00:04:03.060
from a ServiceNow report of closed
findings with their evidence

66
00:04:03.060 --> 00:04:07.020
attachments and a SailPoint
event search for the same period.

67
00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:16.358
Every lane writes the same record, Book
13's evidence record, with seven fields.

68
00:04:16.358 --> 00:04:19.308
The guide shows where each
comes from on these products.

69
00:04:19.308 --> 00:04:22.246
Origin is the SailPoint
trigger and workflow,

70
00:04:22.246 --> 00:04:25.571
the reconciliation finding,
or the catalog request.

71
00:04:25.571 --> 00:04:30.845
Owner and clock are the ServiceNow
assignment, severity, priority and due date.

72
00:04:30.845 --> 00:04:33.995
Approval is the ServiceNow
or SailPoint approval.

73
00:04:33.995 --> 00:04:38.461
Executing identity is the connector's
or spoke's service principal,

74
00:04:38.461 --> 00:04:40.295
or a person's PIM activation.

75
00:04:40.295 --> 00:04:45.157
What ran is the provisioning event, the
workflow version or the session recording.

76
00:04:45.157 --> 00:04:50.283
Result is the re-read evidence, and
closure is the closed record with its hash.

77
00:04:50.283 --> 00:04:56.207
In Pass 1, three of those are empty:
approval, executing identity and what ran.

78
00:04:56.207 --> 00:05:00.795
Pass 1 doesn't actuate, so the record
that would hold them is a later pass.

79
00:05:00.795 --> 00:05:03.820
The export writes each as
null, with that reason,

80
00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:07.200
rather than leaving the
field out or filling it.

81
00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:16.912
The re-read client from Module 6 already
writes the evidence onto each record it

82
00:05:16.912 --> 00:05:21.700
closes: the evidence itself as
canonical JSON, its SHA-256 hash,

83
00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:25.675
the verification time, a
close note and an attachment.

84
00:05:25.675 --> 00:05:27.762
But nothing read the records back out.

85
00:05:27.762 --> 00:05:30.963
Chapter 07 adds the evidence export.

86
00:05:30.963 --> 00:05:35.243
It reads closed and cancelled
findings through the Table API,

87
00:05:35.243 --> 00:05:40.700
under its own read-only role, and writes
one record per finding in Book 13's shape.

88
00:05:40.700 --> 00:05:43.963
Then it runs four checks
on each, reported by name.

89
00:05:43.963 --> 00:05:46.537
Does the evidence still
hash to the stored hash?

90
00:05:46.537 --> 00:05:48.679
If not, it was edited after closure,

91
00:05:48.679 --> 00:05:51.813
or cut short by the
four-thousand-character field.

92
00:05:51.813 --> 00:05:54.275
Does a closed record
carry evidence at all?

93
00:05:54.275 --> 00:05:57.175
If not, the close bypassed
the business rule.

94
00:05:57.175 --> 00:05:59.875
Does the evidence say the
intended state matched?

95
00:05:59.875 --> 00:06:05.030
And did it finish inside its response
window, verified at or before its due date?

96
00:06:08.780 --> 00:06:11.755
The export packages the
period in three files.

97
00:06:11.755 --> 00:06:17.608
Records dot J S O N L holds one record
per line, and each line carries the

98
00:06:17.608 --> 00:06:24.180
SHA-256 of the line before it, so a removed
or reordered record breaks the chain.

99
00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:26.824
Summary dot JSON holds window adherence,

100
00:06:26.824 --> 00:06:30.717
the count of declared exceptions
and the records with problems.

101
00:06:30.717 --> 00:06:36.043
And the manifest holds a SHA-256
for each file and the chain head.

102
00:06:36.043 --> 00:06:40.704
The previous-manifest option continues
last period's chain into this one.

103
00:06:40.704 --> 00:06:43.585
The generated-at option
fixes the timestamp,

104
00:06:43.585 --> 00:06:46.543
so the same records always
give the same bytes.

105
00:06:46.543 --> 00:06:50.529
And verify-dir recomputes
every hash and walks the chain.

106
00:06:50.529 --> 00:06:54.367
That's the check an ISSO runs
on a package they receive.

107
00:06:54.367 --> 00:06:58.118
One caution: the package is
tamper-evident, not signed.

108
00:06:58.118 --> 00:07:02.855
Anyone who can rewrite every file and
the manifest can rewrite the chain.

109
00:07:02.855 --> 00:07:07.582
So, as design guidance, keep each
manifest where the exporter can't write,

110
00:07:07.582 --> 00:07:10.250
and sign it if the program requires.

111
00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:18.975
Window adherence needs care, because it's
easy to measure in a way that flatters.

112
00:07:18.975 --> 00:07:24.262
Chapter 07 measures it the way Book 13
and Book 12's Chapter 12 define it:

113
00:07:24.262 --> 00:07:28.000
closures inside the window,
over closures judged.

114
00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:32.513
It isn't a ratio of open findings,
which improves as new ones arrive.

115
00:07:32.513 --> 00:07:36.735
The kit's fixture has five fictional
closed records, one per case:

116
00:07:36.735 --> 00:07:41.914
one closed in its window, one closed late,
one whose evidence was edited after it

117
00:07:41.914 --> 00:07:44.942
closed, one cancelled as
a declared exception,

118
00:07:44.942 --> 00:07:47.412
and one closed with no evidence at all.

119
00:07:47.412 --> 00:07:49.600
You'll export all five in the lab.

120
00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:53.291
One thing wasn't verified:
the format and time zone of

121
00:07:53.291 --> 00:07:57.325
the date values the Table API
returns on a given instance.

122
00:07:57.325 --> 00:08:02.030
The export parses both common
forms, and Lab 2 checks yours.

123
00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:10.580
Chapter 07 lists the dangers to the
evidence, and the guard for each.

124
00:08:10.580 --> 00:08:14.793
A record edited after closure would
say something the re-read didn't,

125
00:08:14.793 --> 00:08:18.930
so the export recomputes the evidence
hash and reports a mismatch.

126
00:08:18.930 --> 00:08:22.335
Evidence cut short by the
field length can't be parsed,

127
00:08:22.335 --> 00:08:25.818
so it's reported, and the
attachment holds the full copy.

128
00:08:25.818 --> 00:08:31.267
A close that bypassed the business rule is
named: closed without re-read evidence.

129
00:08:31.267 --> 00:08:35.219
A record deleted from the period
would make the package look complete,

130
00:08:35.219 --> 00:08:37.943
but the chain breaks and
the verifier reports it.

131
00:08:37.943 --> 00:08:42.295
Fields filled in to look complete would
claim an approval that never happened,

132
00:08:42.295 --> 00:08:45.979
so later-pass fields stay null with
their reason, and tests pin that.

133
00:08:45.979 --> 00:08:50.230
And if the export ran with the verifier's
role, it could close findings,

134
00:08:50.230 --> 00:08:54.990
so it runs under its own read-only role,
which the kit's call list can check.

135
00:08:58.740 --> 00:08:59.465
Try it.

136
00:08:59.465 --> 00:09:04.115
Lab 1 runs in the kit's servicenow-sailpoint
folder, with no tenant.

137
00:09:04.115 --> 00:09:07.640
Step one runs the chapter's
tests, and eight pass.

138
00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:10.653
Step two exports
October's closed findings.

139
00:09:10.653 --> 00:09:15.578
There are five records: four closed on
a re-read, and one declared exception.

140
00:09:15.578 --> 00:09:19.289
Window adherence is two of
three closed inside the window.

141
00:09:19.289 --> 00:09:23.928
Then it names three problems: one record
closed after its response window,

142
00:09:23.928 --> 00:09:28.033
one whose evidence doesn't hash to
the stored hash, so it was edited

143
00:09:28.033 --> 00:09:32.215
after closure or cut short, and one
closed without re-read evidence.

144
00:09:32.215 --> 00:09:36.097
The command exits with one, because
three records have problems,

145
00:09:36.097 --> 00:09:39.978
and the run says so instead of
producing a clean-looking package.

146
00:09:39.978 --> 00:09:44.474
The one-line Python command prints
three fields from the first record;

147
00:09:44.474 --> 00:09:47.265
on the slide it's trimmed,
and so is its output.

148
00:09:47.265 --> 00:09:49.327
Each is null, with its reason.

149
00:09:49.327 --> 00:09:54.840
Step three verifies the package
as the ISSO would, and it holds.

150
00:09:58.590 --> 00:10:01.778
Step four starts November's
chain from October's head.

151
00:10:01.778 --> 00:10:05.488
The export runs over the same
fixture for period November,

152
00:10:05.488 --> 00:10:09.740
with the previous-manifest option
pointing at October's manifest.

153
00:10:09.740 --> 00:10:14.678
On the slide, its first five lines are
trimmed, because they repeat step two.

154
00:10:14.678 --> 00:10:17.425
The last line reports
November's chain head,

155
00:10:17.425 --> 00:10:21.777
and November's manifest records
October's head as its chain previous.

156
00:10:21.777 --> 00:10:25.278
So a period can't be swapped out
without breaking the next one.

157
00:10:25.278 --> 00:10:28.928
Step five is the test
that matters to an ISSO.

158
00:10:28.928 --> 00:10:32.004
A one-line Python command
deletes the second record

159
00:10:32.004 --> 00:10:35.152
from October's records file,
and then you verify again.

160
00:10:35.152 --> 00:10:40.927
The verifier finds two problems: the file's
SHA-256 no longer matches the manifest,

161
00:10:40.927 --> 00:10:45.840
and the next line's previous hash no
longer matches the line before it.

162
00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:47.528
The package does not hold.

163
00:10:47.528 --> 00:10:50.030
Step six deletes the out folder.

164
00:10:53.780 --> 00:10:59.054
Lab 2 runs the export live against your
own ServiceNow developer instance,

165
00:10:59.054 --> 00:11:02.542
with at least one finding
closed in Module 6's Lab 2.

166
00:11:02.542 --> 00:11:06.667
Create the evidence reader, and check
its role against the kit's call list.

167
00:11:06.667 --> 00:11:10.495
That matters: the export runs
under its own read-only role,

168
00:11:10.495 --> 00:11:15.218
never the verifier's, because the
verifier's role could close findings.

169
00:11:15.218 --> 00:11:17.156
Export live from your instance.

170
00:11:17.156 --> 00:11:20.096
Check the date format of
the due and finished values,

171
00:11:20.096 --> 00:11:23.380
since that's one of the things
the workbook couldn't verify.

172
00:11:23.380 --> 00:11:26.842
Then verify the package,
exactly as you did in Lab 1.

173
00:11:26.842 --> 00:11:29.974
Back it out by deleting
the live output folder and

174
00:11:29.974 --> 00:11:33.030
the evidence reader's OAuth
client, user and role.

175
00:11:33.030 --> 00:11:35.550
The authors haven't run Lab 2.

176
00:11:39.300 --> 00:11:40.687
The federal requirement.

177
00:11:40.687 --> 00:11:45.814
The guide's Gate 7 table maps the tempo
to NIST special publication eight hundred

178
00:11:45.814 --> 00:11:49.028
fifty-three: continuous
monitoring, risk response,

179
00:11:49.028 --> 00:11:52.700
the plan of action and milestones,
and audit record review.

180
00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:57.950
Chapter 07 maps the records and the
package to configuration change control,

181
00:11:57.950 --> 00:12:02.625
continuous monitoring, and A U nine,
protection of audit information.

182
00:12:02.625 --> 00:12:06.258
It also says what isn't
evidenced yet: no approval,

183
00:12:06.258 --> 00:12:10.437
executing identity or run
log until actuation is built.

184
00:12:10.437 --> 00:12:15.757
The evidence an ISSO keeps: the scheduled
run outputs and the five measures,

185
00:12:15.757 --> 00:12:20.218
window adherence and the overdue
list, the exception register,

186
00:12:20.218 --> 00:12:24.336
and the monthly package and its
review; and from Chapter 07,

187
00:12:24.336 --> 00:12:29.226
the records, the manifest and the
chain, verified, and the summary file.

188
00:12:29.226 --> 00:12:32.006
Two controls Book 13 maps to the loop,

189
00:12:32.006 --> 00:12:37.012
A U ten and C M five enhancement one,
aren't in the Moderate baseline.

190
00:12:37.012 --> 00:12:39.701
At Moderate, the chain supports integrity,

191
00:12:39.701 --> 00:12:42.670
and signing the package is a further step.

192
00:12:46.420 --> 00:12:48.395
Check yourself with two questions.

193
00:12:48.395 --> 00:12:54.145
First: in each record, approval,
executing identity and what ran are null.

194
00:12:54.145 --> 00:12:54.833
Why?

195
00:12:54.833 --> 00:12:55.834
The answer is a.

196
00:12:55.834 --> 00:13:00.670
Pass 1 doesn't actuate, so the record
that would hold them is a later pass,

197
00:13:00.670 --> 00:13:02.921
and each null carries its reason.

198
00:13:02.921 --> 00:13:07.020
The record says so, rather than
leaving the fields out or filling them.

199
00:13:07.020 --> 00:13:11.545
Second: how does the verifier catch a
record removed from the records file?

200
00:13:11.545 --> 00:13:12.770
The answer is b.

201
00:13:12.770 --> 00:13:16.342
The file's SHA-256 no longer
matches the manifest,

202
00:13:16.342 --> 00:13:21.432
and the next line's previous hash no
longer matches the line before it.

203
00:13:21.432 --> 00:13:23.782
You saw both in step five of the lab.

204
00:13:23.782 --> 00:13:26.297
The module page has two more questions,

205
00:13:26.297 --> 00:13:31.290
on why the export exits with one and
on how window adherence is measured.

206
00:13:35.040 --> 00:13:35.865
To sum up.

207
00:13:35.865 --> 00:13:38.528
You learned what Gate 7 runs and reports.

208
00:13:38.528 --> 00:13:43.119
You exported a period's closed
findings as Book 13 evidence records,

209
00:13:43.119 --> 00:13:48.038
chained one period to the next, and
verified a package the way an ISSO does,

210
00:13:48.038 --> 00:13:51.153
including catching a
record someone removed.

211
00:13:51.153 --> 00:13:54.903
Before shipping, work through
the Chapter 07 checklist.

212
00:13:54.903 --> 00:13:58.416
And with that, you've finished the
Ticket-to-Machine learning path.

213
00:13:58.416 --> 00:14:02.012
Across seven modules, you
built the loop gate by gate:

214
00:14:02.012 --> 00:14:06.530
the typed task and its contract,
the boundary and least privilege,

215
00:14:06.530 --> 00:14:10.713
one writer per attribute, the
sources and the starting drift,

216
00:14:10.713 --> 00:14:14.227
drift to a task, the re-read
rule, and now the evidence.

217
00:14:14.227 --> 00:14:19.152
What the workbook doesn't build yet, and
why, is at the end of its chapters table.

218
00:14:19.152 --> 00:14:21.000
Thanks for working through the path.
